U2: xy+x-y+1
U3: Prove that:\(3(\sin x-\cos x)^{4}+4(\sin ^{6}x+\cos ^{6}x)+6(\sin x+\cos x)^{2}=13\)","**Let \(s=\sin x\) and \(c=\cos x\).** Use \(s^{2}+c^{2}=1\). First, \[ (s-c)^2=s^2+c^2-2sc=1-2sc \Rightarrow (s-c)^4=(1-2sc)^2=1-4sc+4s^2c^2 \] So \[ 3(s-c)^4=3-12sc+12s^2c^2 \] Next, \[ s^6+c^6=(s^2)^3+(c^2)^3=(s^2+c^2)^3-3s^2c^2(s^2+c^2) \] (using \(a^3+b^3=(a+b)^3-3ab(a+b)\)). Since \(s^2+c^2=1\",Procedural,,,,,Academic,,,,1905,64,0.000442,Skipped full rating by Level-1 gate: gate_llm,1,skip,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
