U9: Solve A calculus limit problem asks to evaluate the right-hand limit as x approaches 0 of ((1−x)^(1/x)−e^(-1)) divided by x^n.","Using the expansion near \(x=0\), \[ \ln(1-x)=-x-\frac{x^2}{2}+O(x^3). \] Therefore, \[ \frac{\ln(1-x)}{x} =-1-\frac{x}{2}+O(x^2), \] so \[ (1-x)^{1/x} =e^{-1}\exp\left(-\frac{x}{2}+O(x^2)\right) =e^{-1}\left(1-\frac{x}{2}+O(x^2)\right). \] Hence, \[ (1-x)^{1/x}-e^{-1} =-\frac{x}{2e}+O(x^2). \] Thus",Procedural,,,,,Academic,,,,2096,64,0.000941,Skipped full rating by Level-1 gate: gate_llm,1,skip,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
