- Anode rays are produced from the anode in a discharge tube when electric current is passed through gas kept at a very low pressure.
- Anode rays consist of positively charged particles because when an electric field was applied in the path of anode rays, the rays got attracted (or bent) towards the negatively charged plate.
- Anode rays are a stream of fast-moving charged particles.
- Anode rays travel in a straight line.

Properties of Protons
- A proton is a positively charged particle.
- A proton is an integral part of an atom.
- The charge to mass ratio (e/m) of proton is not fixed, i.e. it varies.
- The e/m value depends on the gas being used in the discharge tube.
- The mass of a proton is almost equal to the mass of an atom of hydrogen, i.e. \( 1.672 \times 10^{-24} \mathrm{~g} \).
- The charge on a proton is equal in magnitude to the charge on an electron, i.e. \( 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{C} \) but opposite in nature and sign.
The number of electrons in an atom is equal to the number of protons in the atom which makes the atom electrically neutral.
Discovery of Nucleus
In 1911, Ernest Rutherford, a New Zealand-born British physicist, carried out his now famous gold foil experiment to find the arrangement of electrons and protons in an atom. In his experiment, Rutherford bombarded a thin sheet of gold of thickness 0.00004 cm with fast-moving alpha ( \( \alpha \) ) particles, in an evacuated chamber.

Rutherford's Observations
On the basis of his experiment, Rutherford made the following observations.
- Most of the alpha particles passed straight through the foil without any deflection from their path.
- Few particles were deflected at very large angles.

Anode rays: A stream of rays originated from the anode
Protons: Positively charged particles that are found inside the nucleus

