c115523f-36f5-4021-b98a-bc2aa46b14a2,2026-08-18T17:27:36.717Z,30912354,3,"U1: How does the force of gravitation between two objects change when the distance between them is reduced to half
U2: Explain it again
U3: Gravitational force acts on all objects in proportion to their masses. Why then, a heavy object does not fall faster than a light object","**Even though a heavy object feels more gravitational force, it also has more inertia (more resistance to acceleration), so the acceleration comes out the same for heavy and light objects.**

Near Earth, the gravitational force on an object is \(F = mg\). Newton’s 2nd law says \(a = \dfrac{F}{m}\). Substituting \(F = mg\):
\[
a=\frac{mg}{m}=g
\]
The \(m\) cancels, so **both objects accelerate at the same rate \(g \approx 9.8\ \text{m/s}^2\)** (if we ignore air resistance). In real life, air resistance can slow lighter objects more, which is why a feather falls slower than a stone.",Conceptual,5,5,5,10,Academic,,,1.0000,12422,207,0.003057,"The bot correctly and clearly answered all user questions without any failures or misunderstandings. There were no template pushes, context issues, or incorrect answers, and the user did not express any frustration or dissatisfaction.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,paraphrase_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
