U4: \begin{aligned}0\\\Leftrightarrow \end{aligned}","**That snippet is incomplete as written.** 

In math, the symbol \( \Leftrightarrow \) means **“if and only if”** (a two-way implication). It’s used to connect **two statements** that are equivalent, like:
\[
A \Leftrightarrow B
\]
meaning “\(A\) is true exactly when \(B\) is true.”

But your LaTeX:
```latex
\begin{aligned}0\\\Leftrightarrow \end{aligned}
```
just shows a line with \(0\), then a new line with \( \Leftrightarrow \) by itself—so it’s missing what comes **before/after** the \( \Leftrightarrow \).

**Send the full line/equation you’re trying to type (what should be on both sides of \( \Leftrightarrow \)?), and I’ll help fix the LaTeX and explain what it means.**",Lesson_request,4,5,3,8,Academic | Not Math/Science | Specific Named Resource | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,13315,311,0.002921,The bot provides relevant academic content teaching English and Hindi basics with speech-to-text interaction and references a specific resource. There are no evident bot failures or user dissatisfaction. The session is coherent and technically reliable.,0,full_rate,Academic,Language Learning / Translation,same_intent_reask | near_duplicate_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
