U39: Can you explain","**Pulmonary artery and pulmonary veins are the “lung” blood vessels, and their oxygen/CO₂ content is the opposite of what many people memorize for “arteries vs veins.”**

The heart has a **right side** and a **left side**:
- The **right side** pumps **oxygen-poor (CO₂-rich)** blood **to the lungs**.
- The **left side** pumps **oxygen-rich** blood **to the body**.

So:
- **Pulmonary artery:** carries **oxygen-poor (CO₂-rich)** blood **from the right ventricle of the heart to the lungs**.
- **Pulmonary veins:** carry **oxygen-rich** blood **from the lungs to the left atrium of the heart**.

The part “pulmonary veins carry carbon dioxide rich blood from all parts of the body back into the heart” is wrong because **blood from all parts of the body returns by the venae cavae (not pulmonary veins)**, and that blood is **CO₂-rich** and goes to the **right atrium**.",Small Talk,1,1,1,2,Academic | Speech to Text | Slotfill,,Incorrect Answer | Correction Ignored | Gone Exception,0.9500,16862,384,0.004265,"The session is academic with speech-to-text modality. The bot provided multiple incorrect answers, especially regarding the circulatory system, and failed to fully address repeated user corrections, leading to unresolved user confusion. These critical failures significantly lowered the goal resolution and reliability scores, and the user showed implicit frustration by re-asking.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,same_intent_reask | paraphrase_reask | multiple_similar_requests | explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
