U13: How do you verify experimentally that focal length of a convex lens is increased when it is kept in water","**Set up:** Take a convex lens, a lens holder, a meter scale/optical bench, a screen (white paper), and a transparent water tank/tray deep enough to fully immerse the lens (keep the lens vertical in the tank).

**Step 1 (in air):** Keep the lens in air facing a distant object (or the Sun’s image safely onto a screen—don’t look at the Sun). Move the screen behind the lens until you get the sharpest image. Measure the distance from the lens’s optical center to the screen; call it \(f_{\text{air}}\).

**Step 2 (in water):** Now place the lens fully in water (same lens, same orientation). Again form a sharp image of the same distant object on the screen by moving the screen. Measure the new distance from the lens to the screen; call it \(f_{\text{water}}\).

**Observation/verification:** You will find the sharp image forms **farther** from the lens in water, so 
\[
f_{\text{water}} > f_{\text{air}}
\]
That experimentally verifies that the **focal length increases when the convex lens is kept in water** (because the lens bends light less in water than in air).",Small Talk,5,5,5,10,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text | Slotfill,,,0.9800,16460,296,0.003974,"The bot successfully guided the user through creating a study timetable and explained the physics experiment clearly, handling image input and speech-to-text well. There are no detected failures or mismatches, and the user sentiment is positive with no re-asks or complaints. Overall, the session is a well-executed academic tutoring interaction.",0,full_rate,Academic,Study Plan / Syllabus,gate_llm,gate_llm
