Here’s the key idea for square roots:

- \(\sqrt{a}\) means “a number that, when squared, gives \(a\).”
- To simplify a root, **break the number into a perfect square \(\times\) something left over**.

Examples (the same pattern every time):

\[
\sqrt{50}=\sqrt{25\cdot 2}=5\sqrt{2}
\]
because \(25\) is a perfect square.

\[
\sqrt{72}=\sqrt{36\cdot 2}=6\sqrt{2}
\]
because \(36\) is a perfect square.

\[
\sqrt{18}=\sqrt{9\cdot 2}=3\sqrt{2},\quad
\sqrt{8}=\sqrt{4\cdot 2}=2\sqrt{2}
\]

Tell me one thing so I can help the best: **what part confuses you more?**
1) finding the perfect square inside (like 25, 36, 9, 4), or 
