b6524178-bf7b-451e-a76e-76c4618406c7,2026-08-18T14:21:09.802Z,1,10,"U1: Is gf and g/cm³ the same thing or different
U2: Can gf be used in finding relative density and density
U3: Can W be in g/cm³ too
U4: How to convert 1 kgf to gf and 1 gf to kgf
U5: a body of volume 100 cm cube weights 1 KGF in air find its weight in water and relative density
U6: Find weight of water in gf
U7: Find the bridge weight in water in gf
U8: A solid weights 32 gf in air and 28.8 gf in water. Find volume of solid
U9: 1 ml= 1cm³
U10: And 1 gf = 1 cm³","**No — \(1\ \text{gf}\) is not equal to \(1\ \text{cm}^3\).** 
- **gf** is a unit of **force/weight**. 
- **cm³** is a unit of **volume**.

What *is* true (in water) is:

**\(\mathbf{1\ \text{cm}^3}\) of water has a mass of about \(\mathbf{1\ g}\), so it weighs about \(\mathbf{1\ gf}\).**

So you can use the shortcut **only for water**:
\[
\text{Upthrust in gf} = \text{volume displaced in cm}^3
\]
because the upthrust equals the **weight of displaced water**.",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,13938,246,0.003416,"The bot accurately and comprehensively answered all user questions without any factual or procedural errors. There were no failures in context handling, unwanted template pushes, or other issues. The user did not express explicit satisfaction or dissatisfaction, so user sentiment is neutral.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
