### **(i) Speed of light in diamond**

Given:

\[
n=2.42,\qquad c=3\times10^8\ \text{m/s}
\]

The refractive-index relation is:

\[
n=\frac{c}{v}
\]

Rearrange it to find \(v\):

\[
v=\frac{c}{n}
\]

Substitute:

\[
v=\frac{3\times10^8}{2.42}
\]

Keeping extra digits in the calculation,

\[
v\approx1.24\times10^8\ \text{m/s}
\]

Therefore,

\[
\boxed{v\approx1.24\times10^8\ \text{m/s}}
\]

---

### **(ii) Medium in which light travels fastest**

Given refractive indices:

\[
n_{\text{glass}}=1.62
\]

\[
n_{\text{water}}=1.33
\]

\[
n_{\text{carbon disulphide}}=1.63
\]

Since

\[
v=\frac{c}{n},
\]

the speed of light is greatest in the medium having the **smallest refractive index**.

Comparing:

\[
1.33<1.62<1.63
\]

The smallest value is for water. Hence, light travels fastest in:

\[
\boxed{\text{water}}
\]

---

### **How to solve part (iii)**

The wording and values of part (iii) are not clear in the image. If it asks which medium is optically denser, use this rule:

- **Larger refractive index \(\Rightarrow\) optically denser medium**
- **Smaller refractive index \(\Rightarrow\) optically rarer medium**

Please send a close, upright photo showing only part **(iii)** so I can calculate it exactly.",NA,5,5,5,10,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,16357,265,0.003559,"The bot consistently provided accurate, clear, and relevant academic explanations and solutions throughout the session, including handling image-based content and speech-to-text input without any failures or misunderstandings.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,same_intent_reask | near_duplicate_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
