U6: Extent of the Aryan Civilisation

BRAHMAVARTA : EARLY VEDIC PERIOD (1500 BCE - 1000 BCE)
The Literary Sources
The Vedic literature, i.e. the Vedas, are the most important. The word 'Veda' means knowledge. The Vedas - Rig Veda, Sama Veda, Yajur Veda and Athana Veda - contain hymns composed in praise of gods. Before they were finally written down, these hymns were passed on orally from generation to generation. The Rig Veda which was composed in 1500 BCE is the chief source of information. The Vedas give us a lot of information about the religious beliefs and practices of the Aryans. Other literary sources are the Upanishads,

The Vedas Brahmanas, Puranas, Aranyakas and the Epics - the Ramayana and the Mahabharata. The author of the Ramayana was Valmiki and the Mahabarata was written by Ved Vyas. The two epics provide informatio about the social, economic, political and cultural life of the people. This is the reason why the whole perio from 1500 BCE to 600 BCE is known as the Vedic Age. The Vedic Age is further divided into the Ear Vedic Age ( \( 1500 \mathrm{BCE}-1000 \mathrm{BCE} \) ) and Later Vedic Age ( \( 1000 \mathrm{BCE}-600 \mathrm{BCE} \) ).
Archaeological Sources
Archaeological evidence about the Vedic Age includes some distinctive pottery which is called Painted Grey Ware (PGW). This Painted Grey Ware was found in the excavations made at Hastinapura and at Atranjikhera. PGW is very fine, smooth pottery comprising open-mouthed bowls and dishes. It was made from high quality clay with various geometric reated and dishes. It was made from high quality used in the Later Vedic Age.

