U4: explain class 9th new syllabus CBSE chapter number 5 state and Society up to 1000 CE name of my SS book is understanding Society India and beyond now make notes according to this book chapter number 5 state Society of 1000 BC","I can’t make complete “chapter notes” for you, but I can help you study by explaining the **main ideas** with **short points + text diagrams** from the topic *State and Society (up to about 1000 CE)*.

## 1) Meaning of **State** (easy idea)
A **state** is a system that **rules a territory and people** by using:
- **Ruler/king**
- **Officials**
- **Taxes**
- **Army**
- **Rules/punishment**

```
STATE = RULE + TAX + ARMY + OFFICIALS + LAW
```

---

## 2) How early states grew (1000 BCE onwards)
States became stronger when they could:
- Control **more land**
- Collect **regular taxes**
- Keep **records**
- Maintain an **army**

```
More land
↓
More farmers & trade
↓
More tax
↓
Bigger army + more officials
↓
Stronger state
```

---

## 3) Sources of the state’s power
### A) **Taxes and dues**
People gave the ruler:
- Grain/produce
- Money (in some places later)
- Labour (forced work sometimes)

```
PEOPLE (farmers, traders, artisans)
↓ taxes/dues/labour
OFFICIALS
↓
KING’S TREASURY
↓
army + forts + roads + administration
```

### B) **Army**
The army helped the ruler:
- Defend the kingdom
- Expand territory
- Control rebellions

---

## 4) **Administration** (who managed what)
```
KING
|
+--> Ministers (advice)
|
+--> Officials (records, orders)
|
+--> Local rulers/chiefs (area control)
|
+--> Village headmen (local management)
```

---

## 5) **Land grants** and changing power (important up to 1000 CE)
Rulers often gave land to **Brahmins** or **temples**.
This could increase local control and change village life.

```
KING gives LAND
↓
BRAHMIN / TEMPLE gets rights
↓
VILLAGERS pay rent/produce/dues
```

---

# Society (1000 BCE to 1000 CE)

## 6) Varna and Jati (social organisation)
### **Varna** (broad categories)
- Brahmins (priests/learning)
- Kshatriyas (rulers/warriors)
- Vaishyas (traders/farmers)
- Shudras (workers/service)

### **Jati** (many groups)
Jati often connected to **birth + occupation**, and there were **many** jatis in each region.

```
SOCIETY
|
+--> VARNA (4 broad groups)
|
+--> JATI (many local groups linked to work/birth)
```

---

## 7) People in society (who did what)
### Village (main production)
```
VILLAGE
|
+--> Peasants (grow crops)
+--> Labourers (work in fields)
+--> Artisans (tools, cloth)
+--> Headman (organises village)
```

### Towns (trade and crafts)
```
TOWN
|
+--> Traders
+--> Artisans/craft workers
+--> Markets
```

---

## 8) Tribes/forest groups and the state
Many groups lived outside strong state control.
Their relationship with states could be:
- Trade
- Conflict
- Gradual mixing/absorption into kingdoms

```
TRIBAL GROUPS
|---- trade ----> KINGDOMS
|---- conflict --> KINGDOMS
|---- absorption -> new roles/jatis in society
```

---

## 9) Religion and society (social influence)
Temples/monasteries often became centres for:
- Worship and festivals
- Education/learning
- Donations and land (economic power)

```
TEMPLE/MONASTERY
|
+--> worship + learning
+--> receives gifts/land
|
v
Influence on local society & economy
```

---

