U8: Yes and i give you some of slow 2=10,12,13 2 =12,48,60","**Got it — you’re giving LCM questions like these: \((2, 10, 12, 13)\) and \((2, 12, 48, 60)\). I’ll show the Class 6 division (common division) method.**

### 1) LCM of \(2, 10, 12, 13\)

Divide by a number that goes into at least one of them, and keep going until all become 1.

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
2 & 2 & 10 & 12 & 13\\
\hline
& 1 & 5 & 6 & 13
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
2 & 1 & 5 & 6 & 13\\
\hline
& 1 & 5 & 3 & 13
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
3 & 1 & 5 & 3 & 13\\
\hline
& 1 & 5 & 1 & 13
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
5 & 1 & 5 & 1 & 13\\
\hline
& 1 & 1 & 1 & 13
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
13 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 13\\
\hline
& 1 & 1 & 1 & 1
\end{array}
\]

Now multiply the left-side divisors:
\[
\text{LCM} = 2 \times 2 \times 3 \times 5 \times 13 = \mathbf{780}
\]

So **\(\mathbf{\text{LCM}(2,10,12,13)=780}\)**.

---

### 2) LCM of \(2, 12, 48, 60\)

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
2 & 2 & 12 & 48 & 60\\
\hline
& 1 & 6 & 24 & 30
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
2 & 1 & 6 & 24 & 30\\
\hline
& 1 & 3 & 12 & 15
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
3 & 1 & 3 & 12 & 15\\
\hline
& 1 & 1 & 4 & 5
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
2 & 1 & 1 & 4 & 5\\
\hline
& 1 & 1 & 2 & 5
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
2 & 1 & 1 & 2 & 5\\
\hline
& 1 & 1 & 1 & 5
\end{array}
\]

\[
\begin{array}{r|cccc}
5 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 5\\
\hline
& 1 & 1 & 1 & 1
\end{array}
\]

Multiply the left-side divisors:
\[
\text{LCM} = 2 \times 2 \times 3 \times 2 \times 2 \times 5 = \mathbf{120}
\]

So **\(\mathbf{\text{LCM}(2,12,48,60)=120}\)**.

If you send the next set of numbers, I’ll show the same division steps for that set too.",Conceptual,2,2,1,3.333333333333333,Academic | Speech to Text,,Correction Ignored,0.9500,13951,293,0.003450,"The bot ultimately provided correct and detailed explanations for the LCM problem, but the user repeatedly corrected the bot's method presentation, showing initial failure to meet the user's expectations. There are no critical factual errors or context failures, but the repeated corrections indicate some user frustration.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
