04280a69-20ef-4abd-bccf-4d509c1047ca,2026-08-18T14:26:50.684Z,1,4,"U1: Hi there my name is Aradhya
U2: Ok
U3: I am in 8 grade I study my SST Book NCERT chapter 5th name universal franchise and India's electoral system this is the name of my chapter in this chapter I study but I can't understand what is there explain this chapter now in clearly
U4: That is very short explanation explain it in very long","**Universal Adult Franchise** means **every Indian citizen who is 18 years or older has the right to vote**, without discrimination. It is “universal” because it includes *everyone* (with only a few legal exceptions), “adult” because it applies after a certain age, and “franchise” because it means the **right to vote**.

**Why it matters:** In a democracy, the government should be chosen by the people. If only rich people, only educated people, or only men could vote, then the government would mainly work for those groups. Universal adult franchise makes elections more **equal** because a poor person’s vote and a rich person’s vote have the **same value** (one person, one vote, one value).

**India’s electoral system** is the set of rules and steps used to choose representatives like **MLAs (state)** and **MPs (national)**. India is so large that we don’t vote directly for the Prime Minister or Chief Minister. Instead, we elect representatives from our local areas, and then the party or group with enough representatives forms the government.

A key idea is the **constituency**: the whole country (or a state) is divided into many areas, and each area elects one representative. For example, in Lok Sabha elections, each constituency elects one **MP**. In Assembly elections, each constituency elects one **MLA**. This makes elections manageable and gives each area a voice in law-making.

**How voting and winning usually works (simple):** In most elections like Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha, India uses the **First-Past-The-Post** system. That means the candidate who gets the **highest number of votes** in that constituency wins—even if they don’t get more than 50% of the total votes. Example: If Candidate A gets 40 votes, B gets 35, and C gets 25, then A wins because 40 is the highest.

**Who can vote:** You must be an Indian citizen, 18+, and your name should be on the **voter list** (electoral roll). The voter list is important because it ensures only eligible people vote and no one votes twice.

**Steps of an election (big picture):**
- The Election Commission announces the election schedule.
- Political parties choose candidates.
- Candidates file nomination forms.
- Campaigning happens (meetings, posters, speeches, debates).
- Voting happens on polling day using EVM (Electronic Voting Machine).
- Votes are counted and results are declared.

**Free and fair elections are necessary** so that the winner is truly chosen by the people. If there is cheating, threats, bribing voters, or fake voting, then elections become meaningless.

That’s why India has the **Election Commission of India (ECI)**, a constitutional body that conducts elections and tries to keep them fair. It makes sure polling booths are managed properly, voter lists are correct, rules are followed, and results are counted honestly.

The Election Commission also uses the **Model Code of Conduct**, which is a set of rules for political parties and candidates during elections. For example, it stops them from using government resources for campaigning and tries to reduce unfair practices.

**Why this chapter is important:** It helps you understand how ordinary citizens control the government through voting. Elections are not just an event—they are a key way to keep leaders accountable. If leaders don’t work well, people can vote them out in the next election.

