Parallels and Meridians

A globe is a small model of the Earth. When we look at a
globe, we see several countries and places marked on it. To find any place on a globe or a map, we use a system of imaginary lines that run from east to west and north to south. These imaginary lines are known as latitudes and longitudes. The point on the north where the longitudes join is called the North Pole and the point on the south where they join is called the South Pole.
PARALLELS OF LATITUDES

a globe

Look at the globe. Can you see the lines that run in the east-west direction? The ful circles that run from east to west are called parallels or lines of latitude.
An imaginary line drawn midway between the North Pole and the South Pol divides the Earth into two equal parts. This line is the longest line of latitude an is known as the equator.
The part of the Earth from the equator to the North Pole is called the Norther Hemisphere and the other half from the equator to the South Pole is called t Southern Hemisphere.
Facts About Parallels: There are 181 parallels or lines of latitudes. The equator is the most important of all and is also called the Great Circle. All parallels are full circles, except the North Pole and the South Pole, that look like points. The length of the parallels keeps decreasing as we move away from the equator and go towards the Poles. All parallels are at an equal distance from each other. Like parallel lines, these parallels or lines of latitudes also do
not meet each other.
