Extended Learning
It was in the year 1884 that Green wich a place near London, was fixed as the starting point for numbering the meridians.

Numbers of Meridians: The Prime Meridian passes through Greenwich. It is also known as the Greenwich Meridian. It is the starting point for numbering the meridians. It is marked as \( 0^{\circ} \). The longitude of a place is measured east or west of the Prime Meridian. It divides the Earth into two halves - the Eastern Hemisphere and the Western Hemisphere.
A full circle is made up of \( 360^{\circ} \). Meridians are drawn at an interval of \( 1^{\circ} \). There are 180 meridians towards the east of the Prime Meridian and 180 meridians towards the west of the Prime Meridian. This makes a total of 360 meridians. The meridians in the east are marked as E and those towards the west are marked as W. The meridians of \( 180^{\circ} \mathrm{E} \) and \( 180^{\circ} \mathrm{W} \) form the same line known as the International Date Line.
HOW TO LOCATE PLAGES
The parallels or lines of latitude and meridians or lines of longitude form a network on the globe. This network is called a grid. The point at which a latitude and a longitude meet, helps us to locate a place.
