AN INTRODUCTION TO POLITICAL THEORY

WHAT IS POLITICS?
People have different ideas about the meaning of politics. Politicians and candidates contesting elections regard politics as a kind of public service. But some people argue that ""politics is the last refuge of a scoundrel"".

There are others who link politics with personal self-interest, criminalisation and scams. They call it ""dirty politics"". Many people blame politics for their woes and suffereings.

We have thus conflicting images of politics. But we cannot ignore politics. R.H. Soltau. in his work ""An Introduction to Politics"", says, ""Politics is the concern of everybody with any sense of responsibility, for everybody is affected."" According to Marshall Berman, ""Whoever you are or want to be, you may not be interested in politics but politics is interested in you."" Even Gandhiji wrote, ""We cannot aloof from politics willingly"". He also said, ""Politics envelops us like the coils of a snake"".

Politics is in fact one of the unavoidable facts of human existence. Everyone is involved in some fashion at the same time in some kind of political system. Jean Blondel says, ""Politics is a universal activity"". The common citizen is both the subject and object of politics.
DIFFERENT MEANINGS OF POLITICS
- The Greek view of Politics
1. Politics- the Study of Polis or City-state

Politics as a subject of study began with the Greeks, particularly with Plato and Aristotle The term ""politics"" is derived from the Greek word ""polis"" i.e., the city of ancient Greece, th city was also the state- an independent community. ""Politics"", therefore, meant to an ancie Greek, the science of the ""city-state"" or relating to state and government. Thus, in ancie Greece, the science which dealt with state and government or with the political activities of community was also called ""politics"". Plato regarded politics as a bridge between the cit, human being and the city of god"".
