U21: More bigger","**In some janapadas (called *gana* or *sangha*), democratic traditions were followed like this: the state was not ruled by one permanent king. Instead, there was an assembly (often called *sabha* or *samiti*) where many important members met together. They discussed big matters such as making rules, solving disputes, collecting taxes, and decisions about war or peace. The head of the state (chief/raja) was often chosen or approved by the group, and his power was limited because he had to follow what the assembly decided. This kind of system was seen in places like the Vajji (Vrijji) confederacy.**",Small Talk,5,5,5,10,Academic | Not Math/Science | Specific Named Resource | Image Upload,,,0.9800,19407,312,0.004712,"The bot consistently provided accurate, relevant, and complete answers aligned with the user's academic intent and image content. There were no failures in context handling, incorrect answers, or unwanted template pushes. The user engaged positively without signs of frustration or repeated corrections.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
