A velocity–time graph showing a straight line increasing from point A, with dashed perpendicular lines to points C, D, and E. The graph labels velocity in meters per second and time in seconds, and illustrates the relationship between velocity, time, and the area under the graph.","**The image shows a velocity–time graph for motion with uniform acceleration.**

- At \(t=0\), the initial velocity is \(u\), represented by point \(A\).
- After time \(t\), the velocity becomes \(v\), represented by point \(B\).
- The sloping line \(AB\) indicates that velocity changes uniformly with time.
- The acceleration is the slope of the graph:

\[
a=\frac{v-u}{t}
\]

The displacement during time \(t\) equals the area under the velocity–time graph:

\[
s=\text{area of rectangle}+\text{area of triangle}
\]

\[
s=ut+\frac{1}{2}(v-u)t
\]

Since \(v-u=at\),

\[
\boxed{s=ut+\frac{1}{2}at^2}
\]

**The lower part of the graph represents the distance due to the initial velocity \(u\), while the triangular part represents the additional distance caused by acceleration.**",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Mock / Practice Questions | Specific Named Resource | Image Upload,,,0.9500,15909,323,0.003870,"The bot provided thorough, accurate explanations and practice questions aligned with the user's requests and named resources. There are no detected failures or misunderstandings, and the bot appropriately handled image uploads and user clarifications. User sentiment is neutral with no signs of frustration or repeated re-asks.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
