Using the Cartesian sign convention (object in front of mirror \(\Rightarrow u=-10\text{ cm}\)):

- If the image is **virtual** (behind mirror), \(v=+6\text{ cm}\):
\[
\frac{1}{f}=\frac{1}{6}+\frac{1}{-10}=\frac{1}{15}\;\Rightarrow\; f=+15\text{ cm}\;\Rightarrow\;\mathbf{f=-15\text{ cm (concave)}}
\]

- If the image is **real** (in front of mirror), \(v=-6\text{ cm}\):
\[
\frac{1}{f}=\frac{1}{-6}+\frac{1}{-10}=-\frac{8}{30}=-\frac{4}{15}
\Rightarrow f=-\frac{15}{4}=\mathbf{-3.75\text{ cm}}
\]

So **\(-3.75\text{ cm}\)** happens only if the problem means the image is **6 cm in front of the mirror** (a real image), i.e. \(v=-6\text{ cm}\).

