If it’s a system like
\[
2x+y=10,\quad 3x+2y=c,
\]
here’s the method:

From \(2x+y=10\), make \(y\) alone:
\[
y=10-2x
\]
Substitute into the second equation:
\[
3x+2(10-2x)=c
\]
\[
3x+20-4x=c
\]
\[
-x+20=c \Rightarrow x=20-c
\]
Then plug \(x\) back into \(y=10-2x\):
\[
y=10-2(20-c)=10-40+2c=2c-30
\]

So the solution is **\(x=20-c\)** and **\(y=2c-30\)** once you tell me what \(c\) is.",Small Talk,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Not Math/Science | Mock / Practice Questions,,,0.9500,13918,265,0.003443,"The bot correctly provided practice questions and explanations on English sentence topics without any failures. There were no incorrect answers, context issues, or unwanted template pushes. The user did not express frustration or corrections that were ignored.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,gate_llm,gate_llm
