1d0ec0c8-1ef0-4dd0-9551-ad901f4d5128,2026-08-18T12:06:27.343Z,29395678,5,"U1: What is formula for force
U2: How to find final velocity
U3: a constant force at on an object of mass 5 kg for a duration of 2 second it increases the objects velocity from 3 m/s to 7 m/s find the magnitude of the applied force now if the force was applied for reduced duration of 5 second what would be the final velocity of the object
U4: a constant force at on an object of mass 5 kg for a duration of 2 second it increases the objects velocity from 3 m/s to 7 m/s find the magnitude of the applied force now if the force was applied for reduced duration of 5 second what would be the final velocity of the object a force of 5 Newton gives a mass one and acceleration of 10 m/s² and a mass M to and acceleration of 20 m/s² what acceleration would it give both the masses were tied together
U5: a constant force at on an object of mass 5 kg for a duration of 2 second it increases the objects velocity from 3 m/s to 7 m/s find the magnitude of the applied force now if the force was applied for reduced duration of 5 second what would be the final velocity of the object a force of 5 Newton gives a mass one and acceleration of 10 m/s² and a mass M to and acceleration of 20 m/s² what acceleration would it give both the masses vertical buy and give a mass M1 and","**From \(3\) to \(7\ \text{m/s}\) in \(2\ \text{s}\):**
\[
a=\frac{7-3}{2}=2\ \text{m/s}^2
\]
\[
F=ma=5\times 2=10\ \text{N}
\]
So **the force is \(10\ \text{N}\)**.

**If the same force acts for \(5\ \text{s}\):** the acceleration stays \(2\ \text{m/s}^2\), so
\[
v=u+at=3+(2)(5)=13\ \text{m/s}
\]
So **final velocity is \(13\ \text{m/s}\)**.

---

**Now the “5 N gives \(m_1\) acceleration \(10\)” and “\(m_2\) acceleration \(20\)” part:**

If the same force \(F=5\ \text{N}\) acts on each mass alone, then
\[
m_1=\frac{F}{a_1}=\frac{5}{10}=0.5\ \text{kg}
\]
\[
m_2=\frac{F}{a_2}=\frac{5}{20}=0.25\ \text{kg}
\]

Tied together, total mass:
\[
m_{\text{total}}=0.5+0.25=0.75\ \text{kg}
\]
Acceleration with the same \(5\ \text{N}\):
\[
a=\frac{F}{m_{\text{total}}}=\frac{5}{0.75}=6.\overline{6}\ \text{m/s}^2 \approx 6.67\ \text{m/s}^2
\]

So the acceleration when tied together is **\(\boxed{6.67\ \text{m/s}^2}\)**.

One quick check: in your last line you wrote “vertical…”. Is this meant to be a **vertical hanging system** (with gravity/tension), or just “tied together” on a frictionless surface? The \(6.67\ \text{m/s}^2\) result is for the simple “tied together, same external \(5\ \text{N}\) force” case.",Conceptual,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Speech to Text,,,0.9500,13735,269,0.003429,"The bot correctly answers all user questions with accurate physics explanations and calculations. There are no detected failures or misunderstandings, and the bot appropriately handles the speech-to-text inputs. The user re-asks with some paraphrasing but the bot addresses all intents fully, so no correction or context failures apply.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,same_intent_reask | paraphrase_reask | near_duplicate_reask | gate_llm,gate_llm
