ac073723-21fb-4786-bca9-493dfd75eb78,2026-08-18T11:40:55.570Z,1,4,"U1: Ok
U2: Take any five countries UK USA Australia New Zealand tell their climate difference and why is their climate change second question is what is cloud burst third question is rainfall in UP Haryana but why not in Rajasthan
U3: 3
U4: I do not understand","**UP and Haryana get more rain because the monsoon brings them moist air, but Rajasthan often doesn’t get that moist air and also has conditions that stop rain from forming.**

Think of it like this:

- **Rain needs wet air + rising air.**
- **UP/Haryana:** Monsoon winds bring **more moisture** into the north plains, and low pressure helps air **rise**, so clouds grow and it rains.
- **Rajasthan:** 
1) The **Thar Desert** area has **hot, dry air** and often **sinking air** (high pressure), so clouds don’t grow easily. 
2) The **Aravalli hills** don’t block and lift the monsoon winds much (they’re mostly parallel to the wind), so they **don’t force the air upward** to make rain. 
3) By the time monsoon winds reach Rajasthan, they may have **already dropped some moisture** elsewhere, so less is left.

If you tell me your city/district (UP, Haryana, or Rajasthan), I can explain it using a nearby example.",NA,5,5,3,8.666666666666668,Academic | Not Math/Science | Speech to Text,,,0.9800,13255,286,0.003285,"The bot fully and accurately answered the user's multi-part questions about climate differences and rainfall patterns without any errors or failures. There were no slotfills, image uploads, or other modality issues, and the user did not express frustration or re-ask due to incomplete answers. The session is a straightforward academic geography discussion with speech-to-text input noted.",0,full_rate,Academic,Concept Explanation,explicit_frustration | gate_llm,gate_llm
