In a small village in South India, Uma, a poor farmer's daughter, took care of her baby brother, Gopal, after their parents died. When Gopal grew up, he found it hard to get a match for Uma, but a village man loved her and married her for he liked her courage. Uma asked Gopal to continue their family tradition of Lakshmi Puja even after she got married. Years later, Gopal and his wife became rich and held a big Lakshmi Puja every year but didn't invite Uma, fearing she would embarrass them. Still Uma came every year in her old patched clothes and blessed them happily. Eventually, Uma's family became rich too. Learning about her riches, Gopal invited Uma to the Puja this year, she arrived in a fancy carriage wearing fine clothes and ornaments. When offered sweets, she placed them on her jewellery and goldembroidered shawl, and pointed out that the sweets were for her wealth, not for her as a person. She gently told Gopal and Kamala that true love and respect aren't about wealth.
