U3: Q \( 1^{1} \). Write (T) for true and (F) for false for each of the following statements:
- Z
(a) Zero is the smallest whole number.
(b) Every whole number has a successor.
(c) Every whole number has a predecessor.
(d) 500 is the predecessor of 499 .
(e) The predecessor of a two digit number can be a single digit number.
(f) The successor of a two digit number is always a two digit number.
(g) Natural numbers are counting numbers that begin with zero (0).
(h) Natural number 1 has no predecessor.
(i) 3000 is the successor of 2999.
(j) Every whole number is a natural number.

Q12. Fill in the blanks:
(a) The successor of 0 is \( \_\_\_\_ \) .
(b) The line on which we represent the whole numbers is known as the \( \_\_\_\_ \) .
(c) Every natural number is a \( \_\_\_\_ \) number.
(d) The distance between two points on a number line is called the \( \_\_\_\_ \) distance.

Operations on whole numbers
We are already aware of the four basic arithmetic operations: addition, subtraction, multiplication and division on the whole numbers. Here we shall study some properties of these four operations on the whole numbers.
I. Addition of whole numbers

Properties of addition
(a) Closure property: If a and b are any two whole numbers, then \( (\mathrm{a}+\mathrm{b}) \) is also a whole number. i.e. when we add two whole numbers, the result is always a whole number.
Example 1: Let us see with few examples:

\begin{tabular}[t]{|l|l|l|l|}
\hline One whole number (a) & Another whole number (b) & Sum \( (\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}) \) & Is the sum a whole number? \\
\hline 4 & 8 & 12 & Yes \\
\hline 7 & 11 & 18 & Yes \\
\hline 25 & 9 & 34 & Yes \\
\hline 12 & 16 & 28 & Yes \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

Therefore, the sum of any two whole numbers is a whole number. Addition of whole numbers is a closed operation.
(b) Commutative property: If a and b are any two whole numbers then
\[
a+b=b+a
\]

The addition of two whole numbers is commutative i.e. if we change the order of the addends the result obtained is the same.

