A textbook page presents questions about the role of the Indo-Greeks and Shakas in shaping ancient India, the Chola, Chera, and Andhra kingdoms, Indo-Greek territory, and foreign artistic influences in an image. The page includes a photograph of an ancient sculptural figure for a picture-based question.","**The Indo-Greeks and Shakas influenced India’s political and cultural development in several ways:**

- **Political changes:** They established kingdoms in north-western India and introduced new administrative practices, coinage, and forms of kingship. Their rule also connected India with Central Asia and the Mediterranean world through trade and diplomacy.
- **Cultural exchange:** They encouraged contact between Indian, Greek, and Central Asian traditions. This influenced art, architecture, language, dress, and religious ideas.
- **Coins and writing:** Indo-Greek rulers issued well-designed coins showing rulers’ portraits and inscriptions in Greek and Indian scripts. This helped historians learn about their rule.
- **Art:** Their influence contributed to the growth of the **Gandhara school of art**, which combined Greek-style features with Buddhist subjects.
- **Religion:** Many Indo-Greek and Shaka rulers supported Buddhism and other Indian religions. This helped Buddhist ideas and art spread.
