U5: give me the answer for 4.14 and 4.13","**I can see you have multiple questions here. I’ll explain how to do the first one (4.13).** Then you can try 4.14 (and I’ll help once you type the graph values more clearly).

**4.13 (man in a lift on a weighing scale, \(m=70\,\text{kg}\))** 
The scale reads the **normal reaction \(N\)** (apparent weight). Take upward as positive. Then
\[
N - mg = ma \quad \Rightarrow \quad N = m(g+a)
\]
Use \(g=9.8\,\text{m/s}^2\).

**(a) Moving upwards with uniform speed \(10\,\text{m/s}\):** 
Uniform speed \(\Rightarrow a=0\) 
\[
N = mg = 70\times 9.8 = \mathbf{686\,\text{N}}
\]

**(b) Moving downwards with uniform acceleration \(5\,\text{m/s}^2\):** 
Downward acceleration means \(a=-5\) (since up is +) 
\[
N = 70(9.8-5)=70\times 4.8=\mathbf{336\,\text{N}}
\]

**(c) Moving upwards with uniform acceleration \(5\,\text{m/s}^2\):** 
Here \(a=+5\) 
\[
N = 70(9.8+5)=70\times 14.8=\mathbf{1036\,\text{N}}
\]

**(d) If the lift cable fails and it falls freely (free fall):** 
Then \(a=-g\) 
\[
N = m(g-g)=\mathbf{0\,\text{N}}
\]
So the scale reads **zero** (weightlessness).

---

**Now 4.14:** I can’t get exact numbers from the position–time graph in your photo (the curve/levels aren’t clear enough to read precisely), and without those values the force and impulses can’t be calculated.

Please type these from the graph (just the values):
1) \(x(0)\) 
2) \(x(4)\) 
3) What is \(x(t)\) for \(t>4\) (is it constant at \(x=3\,\text{m}\)?) 
4) What is \(x(t)\) for \(t<0\) (is it constant at \(x=0\)?) 

Once you send those, I’ll compute **(a)** force for each interval and **(b)** impulses at \(t=0\) and \(t=4\,\text{s}\) for \(m=4\,\text{kg}\).",Procedural,1,1,1,2,Academic | Image Upload | Speech to Text,,Completion Refusal | Blurry / Unclear Image | Correction Ignored | Context Handling Failure,0.9500,14119,447,0.003623,"The session is academic with physics homework help involving image and speech input. The bot refuses to provide full final answers, citing learning goals, and flags the image as unclear. The user repeats requests after refusals, indicating ignored corrections. The bot also fails to fully leverage the user's stated class context.",0,full_rate,Academic,Homework / Assignment Help,multiple_similar_requests | gate_llm,gate_llm
